#ASSERT NOT (`Schedule 1 puts this MCC in Dining` 5311)
#ASSERT NOT (`Schedule 1 puts this MCC in Fashion` 5812)


-- ===========================================================================
-- TWO WHOLE ANSWERS, PRINTED. Everything above asserts one field at a time;
-- these two print the entire `Rate answer` for a transaction that earns the
-- category rate and one that earns nothing, so a reader can see the cap, the
-- pool, the conditions and the citations together.
--
-- They also carry their arguments INLINE rather than naming the fixtures
-- above, and that is not a style choice. The Catala exporter emits only what
-- an @export decision reaches, so a fixture referenced solely by a directive
-- is not collected and the directive does not become a Catala `#[test]` scope
-- (measured 2026-09-21). Written out, these two become the emitted module's
-- only executable tests. Do not "tidy" them into fixtures.
-- ===========================================================================

#EVAL `UOB Lady's Solitaire earns on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire`
          (Cardholder WITH
             `Solitaire preferred rewards categories`               IS LIST Dining, Family
             `yuu account linked to the card`                       IS FALSE
             `HSBC Everyday Global Account deposits ADB this month` IS 0
             `PAssion membership registered and card linked in yuu` IS FALSE)
          (`Month position` WITH
             `yuu eligible spend so far this calendar month`                     IS 0
             `yuu participating merchants transacted so far this calendar month` IS 0
             `PAssion spend at Participating Stores so far this month`           IS 0
             `PAssion spend at non-Participating Stores so far this month`       IS 0)
          (Transaction WITH
             `merchant name`                IS "SUSHI TEI SUNTEC"
             `merchant`                     IS `Another merchant`
             `merchant category code`       IS 5812
             `transaction description`      IS "SUSHI TEI SUNTEC"
             `description patterns`         IS EMPTY
             `charge kind`                  IS `Retail purchase`
             `amount`                       IS 100
             `currency`                     IS "SGD"
             `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
             `transaction date`             IS YMD 2026 9 15
             `posting date`                 IS YMD 2026 9 15
             `payment channel`              IS `Inserted or swiped at a terminal`
             `credential`                   IS `The physical card`
             `merchant indicator`           IS `Flags card-present`
             `made in Singapore`            IS TRUE)

#EVAL `UOB Lady's Solitaire earns on` `UOB Lady's Solitaire`
          (Cardholder WITH
             `Solitaire preferred rewards categories`               IS LIST Dining, Family
             `yuu account linked to the card`                       IS FALSE
             `HSBC Everyday Global Account deposits ADB this month` IS 0
             `PAssion membership registered and card linked in yuu` IS FALSE)
          (`Month position` WITH
             `yuu eligible spend so far this calendar month`                     IS 0
             `yuu participating merchants transacted so far this calendar month` IS 0
             `PAssion spend at Participating Stores so far this month`           IS 0
             `PAssion spend at non-Participating Stores so far this month`       IS 0)
          (Transaction WITH
             `merchant name`                IS "AXS*SP SERVICES"
             `merchant`                     IS `Another merchant`
             `merchant category code`       IS 5812
             `transaction description`      IS "AXS*SP SERVICES"
             `description patterns`         IS (LIST AXS)
             `charge kind`                  IS `Retail purchase`
             `amount`                       IS 100
             `currency`                     IS "SGD"
             `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
             `transaction date`             IS YMD 2026 9 15
             `posting date`                 IS YMD 2026 9 15
             `payment channel`              IS `Inserted or swiped at a terminal`
             `credential`                   IS `The physical card`
             `merchant indicator`           IS `Flags card-present`
             `made in Singapore`            IS TRUE)


-- ===========================================================================
-- DEFEASIBILITY SITES (spec section 4.5). Where a native DESPITE / SUBJECT TO /
-- exception construct would have collapsed several guards into one rule.
-- One entry per site: the clause, the shape in the text (general rule +
-- exception list), the L4 shape used instead, and what a native construct
-- would have bought.
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- SITE 1 -- cl.2(b) read against cl.32, cl.34 and cl.35.
--   TEXT SHAPE. cl.2(b) grants the Base UNI$ unconditionally: "the reward of
--   UNI$1 for every S$5.00 spent ... on a Card". Three later clauses withdraw
--   it from named classes -- twenty-two kinds of transaction (cl.32), thirty-
--   nine MCCs (cl.34), thirty-nine descriptions (cl.35) -- each drafted as a
--   flat "UNI$ will not be awarded for", with no cross-reference back to
--   cl.2(b) and none to each other.
--   L4 SHAPE USED. The grant is not written where the document writes it. It
--   is written as the OTHERWISE arm of a four-arm BRANCH whose first arm is a
--   disjunction of the three withholdings. The general rule has been turned
--   inside out into the residue of its own exceptions, and cl.2(b)'s text now
--   appears in the module at a place the document does not put it.
--   WHAT A NATIVE CONSTRUCT WOULD BUY. `the Base UNI$ applies` stated once at
--   cl.2(b), with cl.32, cl.34 and cl.35 each written at its own clause as
--   DESPITE it. Order between the three withholdings would then be the
--   language's problem rather than the author's, and adding a fourth
--   withholding -- which cl.36 expressly reserves the right to do, "without
--   giving any reason or prior notice" -- would be a local edit instead of a
--   new disjunct in a rule three sections away.
--
-- SITE 2 -- heading A and cl.4(b) read against the same three withholdings.
--   TEXT SHAPE. The Bonus UNI$ entitlement is granted twice over (heading A's
--   rate line and cl.4(b)'s "You will be eligible to earn Bonus UNI$ for every
--   S$5.00 of spending in your Preferred Rewards Categories") and defeated by
--   the same three lists, which never mention it.
--   L4 SHAPE USED. The withholding arm is placed FIRST in the BRANCH so that
--   it defeats the category arm below it. Priority is encoded as line order,
--   which is the thing a reviewer cannot check against the document, because
--   the document states no order.
--   WHAT A NATIVE CONSTRUCT WOULD BUY. The defeat would be stated once and
--   apply to both grants, instead of being re-earned by the position of an arm.
--   The assertion that a PAYPAL* line at a Dining MCC earns nothing is today a
--   test of arm order; it would become a test of the law.
--
-- SITE 3 -- cl.6(b)'s per-category cap inside its own card-wide cap.
--   TEXT SHAPE. One sentence states a general ceiling (2,700 UNI$ a calendar
--   month) and then a stricter one that partitions it ("with each Preferred
--   Rewards Category capped at 1,350 UNI$"). The stricter limb always governs
--   a single transaction; the general one can only ever bind across categories.
--   L4 SHAPE USED. The general figure is not represented at all. The module
--   returns 1,350 UNI$ as dollars and mentions the card-wide figure only in a
--   comment and an assertion, because `Cap` holds one number.
--   WHAT A NATIVE CONSTRUCT WOULD BUY. Both ceilings carried, the narrower
--   marked as SUBJECT TO the wider, so that a projection could show a
--   cardmember both "S$750 left in Dining" and "S$1,500 across both" without
--   the module having to decide in advance which one the reader wanted.
--
-- SITE 4 -- cl.6(b) against cl.6(c), and cl.6(b) against its own predecessor.
--   TEXT SHAPE. Two overlapping defeasances in one clause. cl.6(c) states a
--   different cap for the Metal card, defeating cl.6(b) by cardholder type;
--   and cl.6(b)'s own Note defeats the FORMER figure by date ("From 1 August
--   2025 ... will be revised from 3,600 UNI$ ... to 2,700 UNI$").
--   L4 SHAPE USED. Neither is encoded. The Metal card has no constructor in
--   the shared domain, and the pre-August-2025 figure is dropped on the ground
--   that this document is the August 2025 version. Both are recorded in prose.
--   WHAT A NATIVE CONSTRUCT WOULD BUY. Less here than elsewhere, and it is
--   worth being honest about that: the missing piece is a Card constructor and
--   a rule-version axis, not a defeasibility operator. Listed because the TEXT
--   has the defeasible shape even though the fix is somewhere else.
--
-- SITE 5 -- a dated row inside an undated list, TWICE IN THIS MODULE.
--   TEXT SHAPE. cl.34 is one table under one operative sentence, and five of
--   its thirty-nine codes carry a parenthetical commencement ("(with effect
--   from 1 August 2022)", "(wef 1 October 2024)"). cl.35 is one bulleted list
--   under one operative sentence, and two of its thirty-nine entries do the
--   same ("AMAZE* (wef 1 October 2024)", "NORWDS* (wef 21 Jul 2024)"). The
--   exception is not to the rule but to the rule's REACH IN TIME, written
--   inline in a cell or a bullet.
--   L4 SHAPE USED. Each list is split by commencement date and rejoined by a
--   disjunction, with every dated limb guarded by a date comparison. One
--   printed table becomes three L4 rules and a three-way OR; one printed
--   bullet list becomes three L4 rules and a three-way OR. A reader checking
--   an entry against the document has to know which of the three to look in,
--   and the two splits do not even use the same number of parts for the same
--   reason.
--   WHAT A NATIVE CONSTRUCT WOULD BUY. Each list kept whole, with a
--   commencement annotation on the rows that carry one. That is closer to a
--   temporal-qualification construct than to DESPITE. The first pass over this
--   module predicted the shape would recur "in at least three places" on the
--   strength of cl.34, cl.32(u)'s "(w.e.f. 19 June 2023)" and cl.35's two
--   dated entries; cl.35 is now encoded and the prediction is discharged
--   rather than merely repeated -- the same three-rule scaffolding is written
--   twice in one file, thirty lines apart.
--
-- SITE 6 -- cl.35's "AMAZE*" against its own "AMAZE*TRANSIT".
--   TEXT SHAPE. Two bullets in one list, one dated and one not, whose matches
--   OVERLAP: every "AMAZE*TRANSIT" line is also an "AMAZE*" line. Read alone,
--   the dated bullet says an Amaze transit ride before 1 October 2024 earns;
--   read with the undated "TRANSIT*" bullet three rows down, it does not.
--   L4 SHAPE USED. Nothing, and that is the finding. The resolution falls out
--   of the encoding for free, because a line carries a LIST of description
--   families and the undated TRANSIT disjunct is reached before the AMAZE gate
--   is consulted. There is an assertion pinning it, which is the only thing
--   that makes the free resolution a checked one.
--   WHAT A NATIVE CONSTRUCT WOULD BUY. Here, nothing -- which is worth saying
--   out loud in a list of six sites that all want one. A defeasibility
--   operator answers "which rule wins"; this is two rules that agree, reached
--   by different routes, and the encoding that makes them agree is the LIST.
